1
00:00:00,432 --> 00:00:03,124
Creating customized versions,

2
00:00:03,717 --> 00:00:05,688
adjusted versions of

3
00:00:06,425 --> 00:00:09,085
open large language models, as you learned

4
00:00:09,085 --> 00:00:11,728
it in the previous lectures, is one of the

5
00:00:12,129 --> 00:00:15,686
big advantages or core features offered by

6
00:00:15,718 --> 00:00:16,327
Ollama.

7
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:19,307
And you saw how you can set the system

8
00:00:19,307 --> 00:00:22,368
message or parameters or even a chat history

9
00:00:22,384 --> 00:00:25,909
and then save that as a new model to reuse

10
00:00:25,909 --> 00:00:27,479
it for future use.

11
00:00:28,377 --> 00:00:32,915
Now besides using set and save to configure

12
00:00:32,964 --> 00:00:33,829
and save a model,

13
00:00:34,471 --> 00:00:36,812
you can also use another approach.

14
00:00:37,694 --> 00:00:40,533
You can create a so-called model file.

15
00:00:41,222 --> 00:00:43,387
And here I'm doing this in Visual Studio

16
00:00:43,387 --> 00:00:45,889
Code, which is simply a text editor. You can

17
00:00:45,937 --> 00:00:48,118
also use a regular text editor. You don't

18
00:00:48,118 --> 00:00:49,642
need a code editor for this.

19
00:00:50,299 --> 00:00:52,256
And you can do this anywhere on your system

20
00:00:52,304 --> 00:00:53,763
in any folder of your choice.

21
00:00:54,436 --> 00:00:56,151
This is also a file without a file

22
00:00:56,151 --> 00:00:58,634
extension. It's just a file called model

23
00:00:58,634 --> 00:00:58,987
file

24
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:03,169
because Ollama has this concept of model

25
00:01:03,345 --> 00:01:05,171
files, which in the end is inspired by

26
00:01:05,268 --> 00:01:06,117
Dockerfiles.

27
00:01:06,886 --> 00:01:08,440
Now, you don't need to know Docker. If

28
00:01:08,472 --> 00:01:10,427
Dockerfiles don't tell you anything, that's

29
00:01:10,555 --> 00:01:11,196
no problem.

30
00:01:11,821 --> 00:01:13,904
The idea behind these model files is

31
00:01:13,904 --> 00:01:17,333
essentially that you can configure models,

32
00:01:17,765 --> 00:01:20,185
adjust models just as we did it with

33
00:01:21,130 --> 00:01:21,627
/set,

34
00:01:22,172 --> 00:01:24,474
but without doing it in the command line,

35
00:01:24,667 --> 00:01:26,970
and then you can load models based on that

36
00:01:27,163 --> 00:01:27,920
model file.

37
00:01:28,451 --> 00:01:30,802
So you can simply configure a model in a

38
00:01:30,802 --> 00:01:33,268
more convenient way here, so to say.

39
00:01:34,820 --> 00:01:37,370
Therefore, every model file starts with a

40
00:01:37,370 --> 00:01:39,921
FROM instruction, which defines the base

41
00:01:40,051 --> 00:01:41,481
model you wanna build up on.

42
00:01:42,376 --> 00:01:46,097
In my case here, Gemma 3, and then 12b,

43
00:01:46,903 --> 00:01:47,467
and then -i.

44
00:01:48,516 --> 00:01:49,815
It-qat,

45
00:01:50,953 --> 00:01:53,486
so using that same identifier I used all the

46
00:01:53,486 --> 00:01:53,822
time.

47
00:01:54,848 --> 00:01:57,381
That will tell Ollama that this is the

48
00:01:57,413 --> 00:01:59,240
general AI model I wanna use.

49
00:02:00,315 --> 00:02:03,312
But now I can override or adjust certain

50
00:02:03,344 --> 00:02:04,819
settings about this model.

51
00:02:05,348 --> 00:02:07,656
For example, as you also see in the official

52
00:02:07,688 --> 00:02:10,253
docs, which unfortunately are only available

53
00:02:10,253 --> 00:02:12,882
on GitHub, you'll find a link to that model

54
00:02:12,914 --> 00:02:15,320
file documentation attached. For example,

55
00:02:15,368 --> 00:02:17,567
you can set parameters with the parameter

56
00:02:17,567 --> 00:02:18,193
instruction.

57
00:02:19,237 --> 00:02:21,612
You can set a system message with the system

58
00:02:21,644 --> 00:02:22,206
instruction.

59
00:02:23,233 --> 00:02:25,834
You can also set more advanced things, but

60
00:02:25,834 --> 00:02:26,973
I'll get back to

61
00:02:27,567 --> 00:02:30,713
one advanced concept, the template, a little

62
00:02:30,713 --> 00:02:31,115
bit later.

63
00:02:31,837 --> 00:02:34,052
For now, I'll focus on parameters, system,

64
00:02:34,550 --> 00:02:35,417
and also message.

65
00:02:36,508 --> 00:02:39,590
So let's do that here. Let's set a parameter

66
00:02:40,088 --> 00:02:43,282
and I'll set the num_ctx parameter, which

67
00:02:43,394 --> 00:02:46,138
defines the available context window length,

68
00:02:47,565 --> 00:02:49,537
and I'll set that to 10,000 tokens.

69
00:02:50,291 --> 00:02:52,599
We can also set another parameter, for

70
00:02:52,599 --> 00:02:53,994
example, the temperature

71
00:02:54,523 --> 00:02:56,351
to let's say 0.5.

72
00:02:57,265 --> 00:02:58,996
The default for the Gemma model at this

73
00:02:59,109 --> 00:03:02,058
point of time is 1 actually. Here I'm

74
00:03:02,058 --> 00:03:04,271
setting it to 0.5 to make it a bit less

75
00:03:04,319 --> 00:03:04,848
creative.

76
00:03:05,906 --> 00:03:08,006
And don't forget, you find the default

77
00:03:08,006 --> 00:03:10,636
params for the model you're using in the

78
00:03:10,636 --> 00:03:13,153
Ollama models catalog on the detail page of

79
00:03:13,153 --> 00:03:16,231
a given model. There under params, you find

80
00:03:16,328 --> 00:03:17,434
the default parameters.

81
00:03:18,268 --> 00:03:20,450
So here for Gemma 3, the default temperature

82
00:03:20,450 --> 00:03:20,786
is 1.

83
00:03:21,316 --> 00:03:23,561
Now I'm setting it to 0.5 here.

84
00:03:24,443 --> 00:03:26,545
And I'm doing that through that parameter

85
00:03:26,545 --> 00:03:28,983
instruction in that model file. And that is

86
00:03:29,079 --> 00:03:30,779
one of the officially supported

87
00:03:30,779 --> 00:03:31,389
instructions.

88
00:03:32,062 --> 00:03:33,746
Of course, you can only include the

89
00:03:33,746 --> 00:03:35,896
instructions you find here because these are

90
00:03:35,896 --> 00:03:37,901
the only instructions Ollama will

91
00:03:37,917 --> 00:03:41,285
understand. I'll also add a system message

92
00:03:41,285 --> 00:03:43,627
and that can of course be anything you want.

93
00:03:44,109 --> 00:03:46,238
It can be a short one, it can be a long one,

94
00:03:46,415 --> 00:03:47,932
like, you are a friendly

95
00:03:48,528 --> 00:03:48,819
assistant.

96
00:03:50,441 --> 00:03:54,090
You will not answer any questions related to

97
00:03:54,253 --> 00:03:55,328
LM Studio.

98
00:03:57,272 --> 00:03:59,172
Now, you should make sure that it's all in

99
00:03:59,188 --> 00:04:01,377
one line. Here, it's getting wrapped by my

100
00:04:01,377 --> 00:04:03,937
code editor, but that's just a visual wrap.

101
00:04:04,259 --> 00:04:06,207
Technically, it's all in one line. And you

102
00:04:06,207 --> 00:04:07,934
should ensure that this is the case because

103
00:04:07,982 --> 00:04:09,808
if you would split it across multiple lines,

104
00:04:10,228 --> 00:04:12,313
this would be interpreted as a new

105
00:04:12,410 --> 00:04:14,991
instruction, but an unknown instruction

106
00:04:15,055 --> 00:04:17,187
because Ollama only understands from,

107
00:04:17,539 --> 00:04:18,934
parameter, system,

108
00:04:19,527 --> 00:04:21,338
and whatever else is specified here.

109
00:04:22,124 --> 00:04:23,518
So that's why that should all be in one

110
00:04:23,615 --> 00:04:25,570
line. Again, here it looks like it's in a

111
00:04:25,570 --> 00:04:28,023
new line because my code editor breaks it

112
00:04:28,023 --> 00:04:30,331
across multiple lines visually, but it is in

113
00:04:30,427 --> 00:04:32,431
one line as you can also tell by the line

114
00:04:32,463 --> 00:04:32,896
numbers.

115
00:04:33,954 --> 00:04:35,781
What's also an interesting setting is the

116
00:04:35,781 --> 00:04:36,823
message setting.

117
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:40,783
This allows you to add messages to this

118
00:04:40,783 --> 00:04:42,900
model file and therefore to the model you'll

119
00:04:43,028 --> 00:04:44,440
build based on that file.

120
00:04:45,434 --> 00:04:47,327
And for that, remember that a couple of

121
00:04:47,359 --> 00:04:50,022
lectures ago when I first showed you the

122
00:04:50,022 --> 00:04:53,551
/save command, I did that to save a chat

123
00:04:53,679 --> 00:04:56,149
history and to reuse that chat history.

124
00:04:57,048 --> 00:05:00,111
Here we can bake a chat history into this

125
00:05:00,111 --> 00:05:00,705
model file

126
00:05:01,395 --> 00:05:03,608
and that history will then be used by any

127
00:05:03,656 --> 00:05:06,415
models we build based on that file, which is

128
00:05:06,447 --> 00:05:07,907
something I'll show you in a second.

129
00:05:08,517 --> 00:05:10,779
Now after message, you should specify who

130
00:05:10,779 --> 00:05:13,089
sent the message and that can either be user

131
00:05:13,506 --> 00:05:16,330
if it's you, if you wanna simulate that you

132
00:05:16,570 --> 00:05:18,030
sent the message to the AI

133
00:05:19,105 --> 00:05:21,159
or assistant if you wanna simulate or

134
00:05:21,929 --> 00:05:25,266
kind of fake that the AI assistant sent the

135
00:05:25,266 --> 00:05:25,602
message.

136
00:05:26,597 --> 00:05:29,212
So here we could for example send a user

137
00:05:29,212 --> 00:05:29,565
message

138
00:05:30,431 --> 00:05:31,506
and say something like, hi,

139
00:05:32,196 --> 00:05:33,752
this is a question

140
00:05:34,908 --> 00:05:38,357
Submitted via the contact form on the

141
00:05:38,438 --> 00:05:38,970
website.

142
00:05:39,953 --> 00:05:42,210
Again, there is no real line break here and

143
00:05:42,291 --> 00:05:44,290
there shouldn't be one. It's just a visual

144
00:05:44,323 --> 00:05:45,906
line break by my editor.

145
00:05:47,066 --> 00:05:48,659
And then I'll add another message here.

146
00:05:49,094 --> 00:05:49,818
Assistant,

147
00:05:51,670 --> 00:05:54,857
hi, thanks for contacting us.

148
00:05:56,258 --> 00:05:58,622
And now that would be some made-up chat

149
00:05:58,687 --> 00:06:01,881
history with which we always start when we

150
00:06:01,977 --> 00:06:02,730
run this

151
00:06:03,355 --> 00:06:05,407
adjusted model version, which we're building

152
00:06:05,439 --> 00:06:06,946
here with that model file.

153
00:06:08,148 --> 00:06:10,072
And that could therefore be one example

154
00:06:10,168 --> 00:06:12,589
model file you wanna build. The question now

155
00:06:12,733 --> 00:06:13,951
is, how do we now

156
00:06:14,496 --> 00:06:17,638
translate this model file into an actual

157
00:06:17,638 --> 00:06:20,395
model we can run with the Ollama command?
